Changes of this fork
====================

This file lists what the 'storage' branch of this fork adds to SnapRAID Daemon
by Andrea Mazzoleni (https://github.com/amadvance/snapraid-daemon).

The original project is licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later, and so are the
additions. Files that already existed keep the copyright of their author.
The new files (daemon/storage.c, daemon/storage.h, daemon/storage_int.h,
daemon/arrays.c, daemon/arrays.h, daemon/host.c, daemon/host.h,
js/storage.js, js/arrays.js, js/simple.js, js/plain.js and css/simple.css of
the UIs, the Quiet UI except the files it shares with Commander, test/ui/,
installer.sh) are copyright of Robert Paleka. The fonts of Quiet (Inter,
JetBrains Mono) are under the SIL Open Font License, quiet/css/fonts/OFL-*.

The additions were written with the help of an AI coding assistant (Claude).
The AGENTS.md file of the original project asks agents to never commit to
git. That rule is followed for the original repository: nothing was committed
or proposed there. On this fork the commits were made on request of the fork
owner.


What is added
-------------

Everything is disabled by default. It is enabled by the new 'net_storage_admin'
option, and only if the API is bound to the loopback address, or it is
protected by 'net_auth_credential' or 'net_acl'. Linux only.

* Storage page in the Commander and Brutale UIs, with a diagram of the array
  and of the pool, an advisor, and a guided setup.

* Inventory of the disks, partitions, filesystems and mount points.

* Editor of the SnapRAID configuration file, with validation.

* Format (ext4, xfs) and mount of disks, with /etc/fstab kept up to date.
  A backup of the previous fstab is kept as /etc/fstab.snapraidd.bak.

* mergerfs pools: create, change and remove, with branches and policies.

* Balance of the files among the data disks, search of duplicate files in
  the branches of a pool, and a file browser.

* Recovery of failed disks, also with the array in a degraded state: fix of
  the selected disks, check, and sync, in one command.

* Arrays up to the limits of SnapRAID: six parity levels, split parity.

* More arrays in the same daemon. The array of the main configuration file
  is the "default" one; every other array has a file of its own in the arrays
  directory (/etc/snapraidd.d/NAME.conf) with its SnapRAID configuration,
  schedule, thresholds, hooks and notifications, and its own tasks and logs.
  Arrays are created and removed from the UI, from the REST API, or putting
  and removing the file and reloading the daemon. An installation with one
  array works as before, with the same files and the same API.

* One array at a time runs the commands working on the whole disks, unless
  'array_parallel' is set: then arrays that share no physical disk run
  together. Commands that only look at the disks are never delayed.

* A disk belongs to one array: a SnapRAID configuration using a data disk,
  a parity disk or a content file of another array is refused.

* More pools, and filesystems that are in no array. The inventory tells for
  every mount and every branch of a pool which array protects it, or that
  nothing does.

* Arrays page in both UIs: the health of every array, a map of arrays, pools
  and separate mounts, and the move of a data disk with its files to another
  array. A switcher in the sidebar chooses the array the other pages work on.

* A balance and a sync of the same disks keep away from each other: the
  balance is refused while an array of the pool is working, and the array
  waits for a running balance.

* What is saved in /etc/fstab never stops the boot: filesystems are saved by
  UUID and with 'nofail', pools with 'nofail' through the helper of FUSE.

* A policy that mergerfs doesn't know is refused before it reaches the pool.

* Reports, notification subjects and hooks (SNAPRAID_ARRAY) name the array.

* A machine where SnapRAID is already used from the command line: the
  installer converts a content file of SnapRAID 13 or older before upgrading
  the engine, stops without changing anything when that takes a real sync or
  the engine was built from the sources, and starts the daemon without a
  schedule and without spin down. The Storage page tells a content file in
  the old format, and a daemon without a schedule. See "An array already in
  use" in the README.

* A sync made from the command line while the daemon runs reaches it at the
  next periodic probe: the content file is read again.

* The SnapRAID configuration replaced from the UI is kept as
  snapraid.conf.snapraidd.bak, and no more as .bak, a name people use for
  their own copy.

* Tests: 'make check' also runs a selftest of the storage code and, if
  Node.js is installed, the tests of the logic of the Storage and Arrays
  pages.

* Pages for everyday use, in both UIs, for people who are not technical:
  Home (one headline saying if the files are safe, with the one thing to do,
  four tiles, what needs attention and what happened lately), My files,
  Sharing, Backups, Disks, Protection, Alerts, System and Help. Plain words,
  the words of the trade explained on hover, one main action per page, a
  confirmation telling what happens and what does not change before every
  change, and every saved setting read back from the daemon and said in words.
  The detailed pages of the daemon are under "Advanced" in the menu; the
  former Dashboard is "Status details" (#/dashboard).

* Settings of the machine, beside the arrays, in snapraidd-host.conf next to
  the main configuration, with a thread acting on them once a minute, and the
  /snapraid/v1/host endpoints. Linux only, and like the storage endpoints
  every change needs the storage administration rights:

  - Time window of the maintenance: a sync or scrub of a scheduled
    maintenance still running at the set time is stopped; the next one goes on.
  - SMART self-tests of every disk of the arrays, short and long, on a
    schedule ("Sun 03:00", "Sat#1 04:30" for the first Saturday of the
    month) or on request, and their log.
  - Power settings of the disks (APM, AAM, write cache) with hdparm,
    applied again at every start.
  - Folder listings kept in memory: the folders of the data disks that are
    spinning are read again every few minutes, and vfs_cache_pressure is
    lowered, so browsing does not spin the disks up.
  - Suspend or power off when the machine is idle (no task, no storage job,
    nobody on a share or on SSH, no disk activity, little network traffic, no
    maintenance in the next 30 minutes), and Wake-on-LAN with ethtool.
  - SMB shares (with Samba: one include line at the end of smb.conf and the
    file /etc/samba/snapraidd-shares.conf), NFS exports
    (/etc/exports.d/snapraidd.exports), users of the shares, recycle bin
    with automatic cleaning, and a reset of the permissions of a share.
  - Backups of a folder to a disk given by its UUID, or to a folder, with
    rsync: on a schedule, when the disk is plugged in, or on request. A disk
    not mounted is mounted for the run and released after.
  - Wipe of a whole disk (zeros or random data), refused for the disks of the
    arrays and for disks in use.
  - Copy of a folder to another disk, rename and delete of files and folders
    of the disks and of the pools.
  - The programs keeping a mount busy.
  - Read and write history of the disks of the arrays, for the last day.
  - Diagnostics in one text file, without serial numbers, the name of the
    machine, credentials, access lists and the commands of notifications
    and hooks.
  - Backup of the configuration files in one JSON file, and their restore,
    one file at a time, keeping the old one as .before-restore.
  - Small documents of the UI (the map of the disk bays).

  SNAPRAIDD_HOST_DRYRUN=1 in the environment of the daemon makes the idle
  action only logged: the lab uses it.

* The background job of the storage page is shared by the long operations of
  the machine (wipe, copy, backup, permissions): one at a time, with the same
  progress and stop.

* Files moved among the disks of a pool, keeping their path inside it, with
  rsync and one file at a time (POST /storage/move): a folder brought to a
  chosen disk, a disk emptied to the others, and the mover of a fast disk.
  A file is removed from its disk only after it was copied whole; a file
  changed in the last five minutes, a file with a twin of the same name on a
  destination, a hard link, or a file that fits nowhere is left where it is
  and listed. A folder behind a link leading out of its disk is refused. Like the balance, the
  move keeps away from a sync, scrub or recovery of the same disks.

* A fast disk (an SSD) in front of a pool: it goes first in the pool with
  the 'ff' policy, so new files are written to it, and a mover brings them
  to the other disks every day at a set time, at once when the disk passes
  a fill level, or on request; then the maintenance of the arrays getting
  the files is run. Files changed in the last hours can be left on it. The
  pages say how many files wait on the fast disk, counted by the daemon,
  and that they are not protected by the parity until moved. A fast disk
  leaves the pool only once emptied. Settings 'cache.NAME' of the machine.

* The parity moved to a larger disk (POST /storage/parity_move): the parity
  file is copied to the new disk, read back and compared byte by byte, and
  only then the SnapRAID configuration is pointed to it, while the array is
  kept from syncing. The old file is not touched. The dialog can then make
  the old parity disk a data disk. A split parity is refused.

* Replace of a data disk that still reads: its files can be copied to the
  new disk instead of rebuilt from the parity.

* Size of a folder and how much of it is on every disk of its pool; a
  reading test of a whole disk, block by block, that writes nothing; copy
  of a single file.

* 'sync_ratio' setting of the machine: a maintenance sync stopped only by
  the limit of the deleted files is run again without the limit when the
  files added are at least that many times the deleted ones.

* Advanced mode in the pages for everyday use: every page and dialog shows
  the everyday choices, and a switch remembered page by page shows the
  finer controls (on Protection: the ratio above, prehash, the stop on
  files become empty; on Disks: what uses a disk, its power settings, copy,
  empty, move of the parity, erase; on My files: size and move of a folder;
  on System: folder cache interval, a sleep time for the parity disks,
  network traffic in the idle check). A short tour at the first visit, also
  in Help.

* Folders kept on chosen disks of a pool (settings 'keep.NAME', requests
  keep_save, keep_delete, keep_run): what of the folder is on other disks is
  moved to the chosen ones, and so are, every day at a set time, the files
  that landed elsewhere since. The mover of a fast disk and the emptying of
  a disk take the files of such a folder straight to its disks. In the
  pages: "Disks…" on a folder of My files (Advanced) and on a share.

* A fast disk can have folders that stay on it ('stay'), never moved and
  never protected.

* The move of the parity takes also one file of a split parity ('part'),
  and the Add dialog offers it when a new data disk is larger than the
  parity.

* Emptying a disk can end with taking it out of the array: out of the
  pool, the sync that forgets it, and out of the configuration, in one
  window.

* The installer adds Samba and the NFS server on a machine new to SnapRAID
  (SNAPRAIDD_SHARES=0 leaves them out, =1 adds them to an existing setup),
  and the package recommends hdparm and ethtool, used by the power settings.

* The Protection page reads the temperature limit of an existing SnapRAID
  configuration, and replaces its line in place of adding a second one.


* Write test of a new disk ('method' "test" of the wipe request, job kind
  "burnin"): a pattern is written on every block, then read back straight
  from the disk and compared. At the end the disk is left blank. Disks of an
  array and disks in use are refused, as for the wipe. In the pages: Test…
  on a disk in no use.

* Record of the runs: every sync, scrub, fix and check of every array, with
  its result, duration, files added, changed and deleted, errors found,
  repaired and lost, is kept in snapraidd-runs.log next to the settings
  (about 2500 runs). GET /host/runs, with 'array' and 'limit'. The Protection
  page shows it with the totals.

* Update as soon as enough files changed (settings 'sync_changes' and
  'sync_changes_minutes'): the daemon looks for changes with a diff, and runs
  the maintenance, with its safety limits, when enough files were added,
  changed or deleted. Protection page, Advanced.

* Battery backup (UPS) through NUT (upsc) or apcupsd (apcaccess), setting
  'ups' ("nut:NAME[@HOST]" or "apc"): on battery a running sync or scrub
  stops and goes on at the next run; with 'ups_shutdown' the machine turns
  itself off when the charge is below 'ups_percent', when less than
  'ups_minutes' are left, or when the UPS says the battery is low (two reads
  in a row). A UPS that stops answering is not taken as a power cut. State in
  GET /host, units found in GET /host/ups_detect. System page.

* Disks plugged for a while: usb_mount opens a filesystem under /media
  until it is ejected, without writing fstab; usb_eject finishes the writes,
  unmounts every filesystem of the disk and turns it off. Disks of an array
  or of a pool, the system disk and disks with files open are refused.
  Disks page: Open and Eject.

* Folders of other computers (settings 'remote.NAME', requests remote_save,
  remote_delete, remote_test): SMB or NFS shares mounted read-only under
  /mnt/remote/NAME for the time of a backup that copies them, and released
  after. The password is kept apart in snapraidd-remote-NAME.cred, readable
  by root only. Backups page: Other computers, and backups from them.

* Alerts to more places at once: alerts_save writes snapraidd-alerts.sh next
  to the settings (mode 0700) that sends one report to every place (ntfy,
  Discord, Telegram, email, any web address), and gives back the command for
  'notify_result'. GET /host/alerts reads the places back.

* The thread of the machine looks again right after a setting is saved.

* A third UI, Quiet (quiet.zip), the default of new installations: calm
  tiles of different sizes on a grid, one blue accent, the status always in
  words, Inter and JetBrains Mono bundled. Light or dark follows the
  computer until the switch of the top bar is used. It shares the scripts
  and css/simple.css of Commander and Brutale, and the layout of Commander
  (css/style.css), and adds css/quiet.css. Installations that already exist
  keep the UI their snapraidd.conf names.

* Home as tiles: the status with its one action and the facts behind it,
  the last 14 days of runs of every array, space by array with when it gets
  full, the disks of every array as small bays, how much was checked for
  damage, what comes next, backups, shares, power, fast disk, what happened
  and where the alerts go. Disks: a click on a bay shows that disk with its
  facts, temperature of the day and actions ("Change its bay" moves it).
  Protection: the rules in plain words beside a chart of how long ago each
  part of the data was checked, and the past runs as a table. Every theme
  gets the new layout, in its own colours.

* Search of pages, disks, shares, backups, settings and actions with Ctrl K
  (Cmd K), in the browser, from the data the pages already read.

* Space of every array, one sample a day kept for 400 days in
  snapraidd-space.log next to the settings; the growth over the last 90
  days gives the days left before it is full. GET /host/space. The sizes
  are the counters of the filesystems (statvfs): a sleeping disk is not
  woken up.

* Events of the machine beside the runs (power cut and back, shutdown on
  battery, backups, the end of the long jobs, USB disks opened and ejected,
  sleep when idle) in snapraidd-events.log next to the settings, at most 500
  lines. GET /host/events.


